Let $b$ be the number of blue ties John bought.
The cost of each blue tie is $40.
The total cost of blue ties is $40b$.
We are given that John spent $200 on blue ties, so $40b = 200$.
Dividing both sides by 40, we get $b = \frac{200}{40} = 5$.
So John bought 5 blue ties.

John buys twice as many red ties as blue ties, so he bought $2 \times 5 = 10$ red ties.
The red ties cost 50% more than blue ties, so the cost of each red tie is $40 + 0.50 \times 40 = 40 + 20 = 60$.
The total cost of red ties is $10 \times 60 = 600$.
The total cost of ties is the cost of blue ties plus the cost of red ties, which is $200 + 600 = 800$.

####800 [end of text]


